{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## HANDOFF — reviewed plan is PASSed and signed; implementation NOT started\n\nSession wrapped on an operator wrap-up directive (weekly usage near limit) after the plan\npassed review and the ticket was claimed, but **before any code was written**. Zero source\nfiles were modified; no Gerrit change was pushed from this session. Returning the ticket to\n`open` so a successor can pick it up. The description IS the reviewed plan — it passed\n`review-plan` on 2026-08-13 (verdict PASS, attested) and needs no rewrite; a successor should\nre-run `rebar review-plan <id> --status` to confirm currency against the then-current base ref\nrather than re-planning.\n\n## Re-measured survey (origin/main @ `2af5a4c773`)\n\n435 `src/rebar` `*.py` files; **46 over 700 LOC**, 22 over 750, 7 at 796-800. The story's\noriginal three-at-799 list is stale — all three have landed or shrunk:\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock.py` 799 -> 730, `_commands/init.py` 799 -> 758,\n`_config_schema.py` -> 677, `_commands/fsck.py` 800 -> 301 (defaceable-icy-antelope).\n\nCurrent worst, with in-flight ownership marked:\n\n| LOC | file | status |\n|---|---|---|\n| 800 | `llm/plan_review/pass1.py` | **Change 1 target** |\n| 800 | `_lib_writes.py` | in flight, Gerrit 1737 — HANDS OFF |\n| 799 | `llm/workflow/completion_banking.py` | unowned; clean seam mapped below |\n| 799 | `_store/push.py` | **Change 2 target** |\n| 798 | `signing.py` | deferred -> `stealthy-appetizing-coelacanth` |\n| 798 | `_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py` | unowned |\n| 797 | `_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_inbound_records.py` | unowned |\n| 796 | `llm/runner.py` | unowned |\n| 793 | `attest/authorship.py` | record exists (manatee) |\n\nAlso in flight and to be rebased over, not conflicting with either target: 1735 (repo-wide\ntyping ratchet, `effete-barbaric-xiaosaurus`), 1730, 1736, 1738, 1742.\n\n## Extraction already staged (not committed)\n\nChange 1's mechanical extraction was fully worked out before the stop. `container_stage.py`\ncomes out at **297 lines** — inside the 200-500 band, above the 100 floor — comprising\n`CONTAINER_CRITERIA`, `_CONTAINER_MAX_WORKERS`, `_too_big_finding`, `build_sibling_roster`,\n`_timed_pairing`, `_run_container`, with this import set (verified sufficient by inspection of\nevery name the moved bodies reference): `logging`, `time`, `ThreadPoolExecutor`, `Any`,\n`evaluate_plan_clarity`, `capabilities`, `LLMConfig`, `LLMUnavailableError`, `Runner`,\n`det_floor`, `passes`, `sizing`, `PlanContext`, and `_submit_ctx` from `.generation`.\n`pass1.py` lands at ~510.\n\n## The two traps a successor must NOT rediscover\n\n**1. `push._git` (Change 2, load-bearing).** `push._git` is monkeypatched at ~25 sites across\n10 test modules (`test_push_strict.py`, `test_epoch_guard_matrix.py`,\n`test_commit_and_push_tickets_branch_heldout.py`, `test_sync_remote_config.py`,\n`tests/integration/test_epoch_guard_reconciliation.py`, others), and 8 of the 9 functions\nChange 2 moves call it. A module-level `from .push import _git` in the extracted module both\ncycles AND binds pre-patch — the tests would keep asserting on a fake while the code ran REAL\ngit against the tracker. The remedy is the project's own precedent,\n`_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py`: pass the calling module in as a `core` parameter\nand resolve `core._git` / `core.logger` at call time. Read its §\"The `core` parameter\"\ndocstring first — it states the same rationale for the same reason.\n\n**2. `_submit_ctx`'s placement rationale (Change 1).** It moves to `generation.py`, and the\nreason matters: the pool worker it launches calls `generation.review_cancelled()`, whose\n`_CANCEL_SCOPE` ContextVar is defined at `generation.py:120` — that is the generation-owned\nstate `copy_context()` must capture. The gate-session vars it also carries\n(`_in_gate_session` / `_active_code_root`) live in `llm/gate_context.py`, NOT in\n`generation.py`. Getting this backwards is what the review blocked on (see below).\n\n## What review-plan blocked on, and why it is worth knowing\n\nTwo remediation rounds before PASS:\n\n1. **P4, priority 1.0** — `Ticket description exceeds the review admission limit` (8051 chars\n   > 8000). This fast-fails with no LLM and is easy to burn a cycle on: the store ACCEPTS a\n   longer description silently, so the limit only bites at review time. Keep the description\n   under 8000 characters.\n2. **E4 + G6, both 0.76** — the reviewer caught a factually wrong justification: the plan\n   claimed `_submit_ctx` \"carries that module's [generation.py's] gate-session ContextVars\",\n   but those vars are defined in `gate_context.py`. I verified the reviewer was right before\n   editing. The mechanism was never wrong, only the stated reason — the reviewer graded the\n   RATIONALE, not just the outcome.\n3. **F1, 0.85** — the \"each change earns LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1\" AC needed an\n   `[operator-attested]` tag, since vote outcomes are Gerrit artifacts an agent cannot evaluate\n   in-session.\n\nOne INDETERMINATE run also occurred (`plan-relation-snapshot-error` / `store-read-failure`)\npurely because a compaction sweep from an unrelated worktree held the tickets-store write lock\nfor 26 minutes. That is transient, not a plan defect — retry.\n\n## Scope judgment recorded, for whoever picks this up\n\nThe plan deliberately does NOT drain all 46 files. Per the epic, the object is the RECURRENCE\npattern. Change 3 — surfacing the near-cap indicator that ALREADY EXISTS\n(`module_size_distribution.near_cap_count`, currently 37) as a non-blocking `::warning::` in\nthe CI Module-size gate — is the part that changes the recurrence, and it is the cheapest of\nthe three. If a successor has budget for only one change, **do Change 3 first**: the two\nsplits buy headroom in two files, but Change 3 is what stops the next instance being\ndiscovered by a contributor who is already blocked. Note the gate lives in exactly ONE place,\nthe reusable `.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml` that both `gerrit-verify.yaml` and\n`test.yml` call — `docs/architecture.md` describes it as living in the two callers, which is\ntrue of the callers, not the step.\n\n`completion_banking.py` (799) was outlined but not fully mapped; its `plan_recovery_batches` /\n`successor_instructions` / `resolve_verifier_slot` cluster (lines ~94-321, ~230 LOC) looks like\na clean single-seam extraction with no monkeypatching on the module's own symbols. That is the\nnext-cheapest split after these two if the sweep continues.\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786655663246152001,"uuid":"57e2d981-30fa-423e-8c55-346d3130f0b3"}